      en: "The Mabaruma Cocoa Project, initiated in 2012 in Barima-Waini, Guyana, is a community-based initiative covering 200 hectares. It involves 150 smallholder farmers cultivating Trinitario and Criollo cocoa varieties. Annual production reaches about 50 tons of dried beans, with a portion processed into artisanal chocolate. The project provides training, seedlings, and promotes agroforestry practices, intercropping cocoa with shade trees such as rosewood and cedar. In 2023, 10,000 shade trees were planted. Cocoa is exported to specialty chocolate makers in Europe. The project aims to reduce reliance on gold mining and improve rural livelihoods. It has achieved organic certification for a subset of farms.",
